00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:21.550
<v Orlando>Welcome to <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang>. This is lesson number 7. We're
going to talk about indirect pronouns and the word <lang pt-BR>para</lang>.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.550
<v Michelle>What is that, actually?

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.460
<v Orlando>Well, you know, this is going to be a fun lesson because it's one
of these great differences again between Spanish and Portuguese,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.300
<v Orlando>José Luis is going to have to defend Spanish this whole lesson.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:33.800
<v José Luis>I will.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:39.300
<v Orlando>The idea is this in this lesson: Spanish speakers
use pronouns a lot, <lang es>me</lang>, <lang es>te</lang>, <lang es>le</lang>, <lang es>nos</lang>, all those sorts.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.800
<v Valdo>The double, the pronoun.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:44.090
<v Orlando>In Portuguese you don't. We drop them out a lot.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.300
<v Orlando>Right.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:52.550
<v Valdo>because we speak very clearly and we avoid ambiguity

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:53.390
<v José Luis>Wow.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.630
<v Michelle>Because it's more complicated.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.300
<v Valdo>It's more complicated.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:56.590
<v Valdo>And for a Brazilian mind, actually, the Spanish is going
to be redundant, because it has a lot of information.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:02.540
<v Michelle>In Spanish.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:10.900
<v Valdo>And for us, for example, the indirect pronoun, we tend to
use prepositions, like <lang pt-BR>para ele</lang>, <lang pt-BR>para ela</lang>, <lang pt-BR>para nós</lang>, simple.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:11.900
<v Orlando>Right.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.620
<v Valdo>We don't have the clitic doubling.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:14.620
<v Orlando>Right.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.300
<v Orlando>So, ooh, clitic doubling, that was a neat word.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.280
<v Valdo>We have no clitic doubling in Portuguese.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:21.050
<v José Luis>No.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:26.984
<v Orlando>Now, what we're going to do then, probably to get us
going, let's remind everybody what an indirect object is.

00:01:26.984 --> 00:01:30.300
<v Orlando>And that's the part of the sentence that answers the question…

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.050
<v Michelle>To whom?

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.250
<v Orlando>So, "I gave the apple to Mary".

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.300
<v Orlando>"To Mary" is the indirect object.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.050
<v Valdo>Exactly. So it's a preposition, <lang pt-BR>para Maria</lang>.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.959
<v Orlando>And before we read the dialogue, let's look at a couple
of sentences comparing in Spanish and Portuguese,

00:01:42.959 --> 00:01:45.920
<v Orlando>just so we can get a sense of what the dialogue is going to say.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.180
<v Orlando>If in Spanish I wanted to say, did you give Carlos the letter?

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.550
<v Orlando>I think, and José Luis, correct me, that would be...

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.770
<v Orlando>I think, and José Luis, correct me, that
would be <lang es>¿le diste a Carlos la carta?</lang>

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:58.320
<v José Luis><lang es>Correcto.</lang>

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.050
<v Orlando>And the answer would be something like…

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.800
<v José Luis><lang es>Sí, se la di ayer.</lang>

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.050
<v Orlando>And so you've got to take that…

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.050
<v José Luis><lang es>Se la di.</lang>

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.800
<v Orlando>Now, don't make it a big grammar lesson,
but what is the <lang es>se la di</lang>, José Luis?

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.500
<v José Luis>the receiver and the it.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:23.890
<v Orlando>The receiver and the it. So the <lang es>le la</lang> becomes a <lang es>se la</lang>, and you
put it together, make a <lang es>se la di</lang>, means "I gave it to him".

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.050
<v Orlando>So that very sentence,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.250
<v José Luis><lang es>¿Le diste a Carlos la carta?</lang>

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.750
<v José Luis><lang es>Sí, se la di ayer.</lang>

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.000
<v Orlando>In Portuguese, you'd probably say something more like what?

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.350
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Você deu a carta para Carlos?</lang>

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:36.890
<v Orlando>Right.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.550
<v Orlando>So literally, "you gave the letter
to Carlos", and the answer would be…

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:43.800
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Dei sim.</lang>

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:46.800
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Dei sim.</lang>

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.800
<v Michelle>Or <lang pt-BR>dei</lang>.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.300
<v Michelle>Just <lang pt-BR>dei</lang>.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:52.300
<v Valdo>That's very short.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.300
<v Valdo>It's all there.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.300
<v Michelle>All the information is there.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:55.300
<v Orlando>That's the game here.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:03:04.300
<v Orlando><lang pt-BR>Você deu a carta para Carlos?</lang> <lang pt-BR>Dei.</lang> There's none of this
<lang es>se la di</lang>, <lang es>se lo di</lang>, <lang es>se nos di</lang>, all that sort of stuff.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.300
<v Valdo>No.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:06.300
